course: dictionary
lesson: 9693
title: Suffragists and the right to vote
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A suffragist is a person who campaigns for the right to vote, especially for women. The word comes from suffrage, which means the right to vote. «Suffragists campaigned for votes for women with petitions, meetings and peaceful marches, and Millicent Fawcett led them in Britain.»
explain: Look at the clues in that sentence. Campaigned tells you they worked hard to change something, and peaceful tells you how they did it. Petitions are lists of names asking for a change, and marches are walks where many people go together to be seen and heard. So a suffragist was someone who tried to change the law in a calm, lawful way.
explain: Suffragists and suffragettes both wanted women to vote, but they did not always act the same way. «Suffragists campaigned peacefully with petitions and marches, while the suffragettes in Britain chose bolder deeds, chaining themselves to railings and going to prison.» Suffragist is the word for the peaceful campaigners, so do not mix the two up.
fact: A suffragist is a person who campaigns for the right to vote.
fact: Suffrage means the right to vote.
fact: Suffragists used peaceful methods such as petitions, meetings and marches.
fact: In Britain, Millicent Fawcett led the suffragists, and Emmeline Pankhurst led the suffragettes.
fact: New Zealand was the first self-governing country to give women the vote in national elections, in 1893, after suffragist Kate Sheppard's petition.
exercise: «Suffragists campaigned for votes for women with petitions, meetings and peaceful marches.» What did suffragists use to campaign? | Secret letters to the king | *Petitions, meetings and peaceful marches | Breaking windows and chaining themselves to railings | Fireworks in the street
exercise: Suffragist comes from suffrage. What does suffrage mean? | The right to a day off work | *The right to vote | The right to travel abroad | The right to a free school
exercise: «Suffragists campaigned peacefully with petitions and marches, while the suffragettes in Britain chose bolder deeds.» Which group used peaceful methods? | Suffragettes | Both groups, always chaining themselves to railings | *Suffragists | Neither group
test: Kate Sheppard was a suffragist in New Zealand, and her petition helped women win the vote in 1893. What helped win the vote in New Zealand? | *A petition | A battle at sea | A royal wedding | A new railway
test: Susan B. Anthony and Elizabeth Cady Stanton were suffragists in which country? | Australia | Britain | *The USA | Canada
exam: «Your great-great-gran may not have been allowed to vote just because she was a woman, until campaigners called suffragists won that right.» What did suffragists campaign for? | *The right to vote | A bigger house | Free dinners at school | Shorter working hours
